# Swedish grammar -- manifest.
#
# Second language for the engine, mirroring the English fragment's structure
# (see languages/english/en.gram) but exercising new ground: two genders
# (en/utrum vs ett/neutrum) with adjective agreement, suffixal + double
# definiteness (hund -> hunden -> hundarna; den stora hunden), and -- where
# Swedish is simpler -- no present-tense subject-verb agreement.
#
# Syntax of this DSL is documented in en.gram; the same parser serves both.
#
# Features
# --------
#   num     = sg | pl            number
#   gender  = utr | neu          common (en-words) vs neuter (ett-words)
#   def     = indef | def        definiteness (suffixal on nouns)
#   case    = nom | acc          pronoun case (subject vs object)
#   article = opt | req          whether a bare noun needs an article (see note)
#   degree  = pos | comp | sup   adjective comparison
#   tense   = pres | past        finite tense
#   vform   = bare | fin | sup   verb form (infinitive / finite / supine)
#   req     = bare | sup         complement form an auxiliary selects
#
# Note on `article`: Swedish bare nouns are grammatical when definite ("hunden",
# "hundarna") or indefinite plural ("hundar"), but an indefinite *singular* count
# noun needs an article ("en hund", not "*hund"). That licensing condition is a
# disjunction (def=def OR num=pl) which atomic unification can't state, so each
# noun form carries a precomputed atomic `article` (req only on indefinite
# singulars) and NP -> N is a plain equality check. Same device as English `vagr`.
# Future: revisit when a disjunctive value type exists.
#
# Note on adjective strong/weak: Swedish adjectives have a strong (indefinite)
# and a weak (definite) declension. Rather than a separate feature, the contrast
# rides on `def`: strong forms carry def=indef, the weak form def=def. Adjective
# definiteness then unifies with the article's and noun's, so "en stor hund"
# (all indef) and "den stora hunden" (all def) concord, while "den stor hunden"
# (strong adjective in a definite phrase) clashes.

%feature num     : sg | pl
%feature gender  : utr | neu
%feature def     : indef | def
%feature case    : nom | acc
%feature article : opt | req
%feature degree  : pos | comp | sup
%feature tense   : pres | past
%feature vform   : bare | fin | sup
%feature req     : bare | sup
%feature lemma   : *

# Swedish parses from S and splits on whitespace. No clitics: negation "inte" is
# a free word, and there are no English-style contractions.
%start S
%tokenizer whitespace

%include closed.gram
%include adjectives.gram
%include morph.gram
%import  lexicon.tsv
%include syntax.gram
